To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH = Σ ΔH°(products) - Σ ΔH°(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation are:

ΔH°(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the formula:

ΔH = ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s)) - [ΔH°(CaO(s)) + ΔH°(H2O(l))]

ΔH = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - [(-635.1 kJ/mol) + (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-920.9 kJ/mol)

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol + 920.9 kJ/mol

ΔH = -65.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is -65.3 kJ/mol.
