   \]
   The standard reduction potentials are:
   - \( \text{Ag}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Ag} \quad \text{E}^\circ = +0.7996 \text{ V} \)
   - \( \text{Cu}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Cu} \quad \text{E}^\circ = +0.342 \text{ V} \)

   The standard cell potential is:
   \[
   \text{E}^\circ = \text{E}^\circ(\text{anode}) - \text{E}^\circ(\text{cathode}) = 0.342 \text{ V} - (-0.342 \text{ V}) = 0.342 \text{ V} - 0.7996 \text{ V} = -0.4576 \text{ V}
   \]
   (Note: The cathode is where reduction occurs, and the anode is where oxidation occurs.)

2. **Calculate the non-standard cell potential (Ecell)**
   Using the Nernst equation:
   \[
   \text{E} = \text{E}^\circ - \frac{RT}{nF} \ln \left( \frac{\text{Ag}^+}{Cu^+} \right)
