The concentration of hydonium ions \([H_3O^+]\) is given as \(10^{-4}\) M. The concentration of hydroxide ions \([OH^-]\) can be calculated using the ion product of water (\(K_w\)):
\[
K_w = [H_3O^+][OH^-] = 10^{-14} \text{ M}^2
\]
Substituting the given \([H_3O^+]\) value:
\[
10^{-14} = 10^{-4}[OH^-]
\]
\[
[OH^-] = \frac{10^{-14}}{10^{-4}} = 10^{-10} \text{ M}
\]
The concentration of hydroxide ions is \(10^{-10}\) M. To determine the nature of the solution, we compare the concentrations of \([H_3O^+]\) and \([OH^-]\):
- If \([H_3O^+] > [OH^-]\), the solution is acidic.
- If \([H_3O^+] < [OH^-\)], the solution is basic.
- If \([H_3O^+] = [OH^-\]), the solution is neutral.

Since \([H_3O^+] = 10^{-4} \text{ M}\) and \([OH^-] = 10^{-10} \text{ M}\), we find that \([H_3O^+] > [OH^-\]). Therefore, the solution is acidic.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

