## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the lattice energy of CaCl2 given that the distance between the Ca2+ and Cl- ions in the crystal lattice is 0.231 nm and the Born-Lande equation constant is 2.21 x 10^-19 J nm.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the lattice energy of CaCl2, we can use the Born-Lise equation:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi \text{B} \text{Z}^2}{4\pi \varepsilon_0} \frac{1}{r}
\]

where:
- \(\text{B}\) is the Born-Lande constant (\(2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J nm}\))
- \(\text{Z}\) is the sum of the charges of the ions (for \(Ca^{2+}\) and \(Cl^-\), \(Z = 2 + 1 = 3\))
- \(r\) is the distance between the ions (\(0.211 \, \text{nm}\))
- \(\epsilon_0\) is the vacuum permittivity (\(8.85 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{F/m}\))

Substituting the given values:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi \times 2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J nm} \times (3)^2}{4\pi \times 8.85 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{F/m}} \times \frac{1}{0.211 \, \text{nm}}
\]

Simplifying:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{2.21 \times 10^{-19} \times 9}{8.85 \times 10^{-12}} \times \frac{1}{0.211} \, \text{J}
\]

\[
\text{Latt Energy} = \frac{19.89 \times 10^{-19}}{8.85 \times 10^{-12}} \times 4.73 \, \text{J}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = 2.24 \times 10^{-7} \times 4.73 \, \text{J}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = 1.06 \times 10^5 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the lattice energy of CaCl₂ is \(\boxed{1.06 \times 10^5 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

