$$P(n) = \binom{12}{n} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12-n} = \binom{12}{n} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}$$
Here, $\binom{12}{n}$ is the binomial coefficient, which is the number of ways to choose $n$ heads from 12 flips.

1. For 9 heads:
$$P(9) = \binom{12}{9} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{12!}{9!3!} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{12 \times 11 \times 10}{3 \times 2 \times 1} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 220 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}$$

2. For 10 heads:
$$P(10) = \binom{12}{10} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{12!}{10!2!} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{12 \times 11}{2 \times 1} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 66 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}$$

3. For 11 heads:
$$P(11) = \binom{12}{11} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{12!}{11!1!} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 12 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}$$

4. For 12 heads:
$$P(12) = \binom{1}{2}{12} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 1 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}$$

The probability of getting at least 9 heads is the sum of these probabilities:
$$P(\text{at least 9 heads}) = P(9) + P(10) + P(11) + P(12) = \frac{220 + 66 + 12 + 1}{2^{12}} = \frac{300}{4096} = \frac{75}{1024}$$

