","To find the length of the train, we use the formula:
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
\]
First, we need to convert the speeds to meters per second (m/s).
\[
\text{Joger speed} = 9 \text{ km/hr} = 9 \times \frac{1000}{3600} = 2.5 \text{ m/s}
\]
\[
\text{Train speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000}{3600} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]
The train is moving at 12.5 m/s and the joger is moving at 2.5 m/s in the same direction. The relative speed of the train with respect to the joger is:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 12.5 - 2.5 = 10 \text{ m/s}
\]
The train takes 25 seconds to pass the joger. During this time, the train must cover the distance of 150 m (the initial distance between the jogger and the train) and the length of the train. Let \(L\) be the length of the train in meters. The total distance covered by the train is \(150 + L\) m. Using the formula for time:
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
\]
\[
25 = \frac{150 + L}{10}
\]
Multiply both sides by 10:
\[
25 \times 10 = 150 + L
\]
\[
250 = 150 + L
\]
Subtract 150 from both sides:
\[
L = 250 - 150 = 100 \text{ m}
\]
Thus, the length of the train is \(\boxed{100 \text{ m}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
