</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Let $d$ represent the cost of Ann traveling 150 kilometers. We establish the proportion using the excess fare beyond the initial 10 kilometers. For the initial trip of 100 kilometers, the excess distance covered is $100 - 10 = 90$ kilometers, which costs €150 - €90 = €60.

Calculating the cost per kilometer beyond the initial 10 kilometers:
\[
\text{Cost per kilometer} = \frac{\text{Excess fare}}{\text{Excess distance}} = \frac{€60}{90 \text{ km}} = \frac{2}{3} \text{ €/km}
\]

For a trip of 150 kilometers, the excess distance beyond the first 10 kilometers is $150 - 10 = 140$ kilometers. Thus, the fare for this part of the trip is:
\[
\text{Fare beyond 10 km} = 140 \times \frac{2}{3} \text{ €/km} = \frac{280}{3} \text{ €} = 93.33 \text{ €}
\]

Adding the minimum fare:
\[
d = \text{base rate} + \text{fare beyond 10 km} = 90 \text{ €} + 93.33 \text{ €} = \boxed{183.33 \text{ €}}
\]
