<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is calculated using Newton's second law:  
\[ F = m a \]  
The force on the proton is given by the electric field:  
\[ F = q E = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \]  
Substituting into Newton's second law:  
\[ 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} = (1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}) a \]  
\[ a = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed is \(0 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v = v_0 + a t \]  
where \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\), and \(a = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\).  
\[ 35,000 \, \text{m/s} = 0 + (4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2) t \]  
\[ t = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.2 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s} \]  

Final answers:  
(a) \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\)  
