\[
e = \frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_{\text{in}}}
\]
where \( v_{\text{out}} \) is the relative velocity of the ball after the collision and \( v_{\text{in}} \) is the relative velocity of the ball before the collision.

In this case, the coefficient of restitution is given as \( e = \frac{1}{3} \). The ball rebounds at the same angle as its angle of incidence, so the angle of incidence and the angle of exit are the same.

The kinetic energy of the ball before the collision is:
\[
KE_{\text{initial}} = \frac{1}{2} m v^2
\]
where \( m \) is the mass of the ball and \( v \) is its velocity before the collision.

After the collision, the ball rebounds at an angle equal to the angle of incidence. The velocity of the ball after the collision is:
\[
v_{\text{out}} = e v_{\text{in}} = \frac{1}{3} v
\]
The kinetic energy of the ball after the collision is:
\[
KE_{\text{final}} = \frac{1}{2} m v_{\text{out}}^2 = \frac{1}{2} m \left( \frac{1}{3} v \right)^2 = \frac{1}{2} m \cdot \frac{1}{9} v^2 = \frac{1}{9} \left( \frac{1}{2} m v^2 \right) = \frac{1}{9} KE_{\text{initial}}
\]

The fraction of kinetic energy lost is:
\[
\text{Fraction lost} = 1 - \frac{KE_{\text{final}}}{KE_{\text{initial}}} = 1 - \frac{\frac{1}{9} KE_{\text{initial}}}{KE_{\text{initial}}} = 1 - \frac{1}{9} = \frac{8}{9}
\]

Thus, the fraction of kinetic energy lost during the collision is \(\boxed{\dfrac{8}{9}}\).
