<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation formula states that the time experienced by an moving object is shorter than the time experienced by a stationary observer. The formula is:

Δt' = Δt * sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where Δt' is the time experienced by the moving object, Δt is the time experienced by the stationary observer, v is the relative velocity between the two objects, and c is the speed of light.

In this case, the clock in the cockpit is the moving object, and the clock on Earth is the stationary observer. We are given that Δt' = 3 years (the time on the cockpit clock), and v = 0.6c. We can use the formula to find Δt, the time experienced by the clock on Earth:

3 years = Δt * sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
3 years = Δt * sqrt(1 - 0.36)
3 years = Δt * sqrt(0.64)
3 years = Δt * 0.8
Δt = 3 years / 0.8
Δt = 3.75 hours

Now, the time dilation formula also states that the time experienced by the moving object is shorter than the time experienced by a stationary observer. Since the clock in the cargo bay is on the same spaceship, it will experience the same time dilation as the clock in the cockpit. Therefore, the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth will also be 3 years.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

