","The lifetime of a particle is related to its energy through the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. The uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) is related to the lifetime (\(\tau\)) by:

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\13}{2\pi \tau}
\]

For the Σ⁺ particle, the rest energy is 1385 MeV and the lifetime is \(2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s. Plugging these values into the uncertainty relation:

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{13}{2\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} \approx 1.03 \times 10^{-22} \text{ MeV}
\]

This means the energy of the Σ⁺ particle is subject to a small spread of about \(1.03 \times 10^{-23} \text{ MeV}\).

The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be within this uncertainty. Therefore, the measured value of the rest energy would be within:

\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 1.03 \times 10^{-23} \text{ MeV}
\]

However, the uncertainty is very small compared to the rest energy.

